To Weland came woes and wearisome trial,
And cares oppressed the constant earl;
His lifelong companions were pain and sorrow, …

. . . . . . . . . . was broken;
He bade the young barons abandon their horses,
To drive them afar and dash quickly forth, …

Before thou wast born, there was built thee a house;
For thee was a mould meant ere thy mother bore thee;
They have not made it ready nor reckoned its depth; …

With my mouth I am master of many a language;
Cunningly I carol; I discourse full oft
In melodious lays; loud do I call, …

I'm found under water, held fast by my mouth;
Swirl of the sea-tides goes sweeping beneath me.
Fathom-deep sunk under surges, I grew. …

I war with the wind, with the waves I wrestle;
I must battle with both when the bottom I seek,
My strange habitation by surges o'er-roofed. …

I'm prized by men, in the meadows I'm found,
Gathered on hill-sides, and hunted in groves;
From dale and from down, by day I am brought. …

Mirie it is while sumer ilast
  With fugheles song;
Oc nu necheth windes blast …

Hit wes upon a Scere Þorsday þat vre Louerd aros;
Ful milde were þe wordes He spec to Iudas:
 …

Wynter wakeneth al my care,
Nou this leves waxeth bare;
Ofte I sike ant mourne sare …

Flee fro the prees, and dwelle with sothfastnesse,
Suffyce unto thy good, though hit be smal;
For hord hath hate, and climbing tikelnesse, …

A blisful lyf, a paisible and a swete
Ledden the peples in the former age;
They helde hem payed of fruites, that they ete, …

Madame, ye ben of al beautè shryne
As fer as cercled is the mappemounde;
For as the cristal glorious ye shyne, …

Loke wel aboute, ye that lovers be;
Lat nat your lustes lede you to dotage;
Be nat enamoured on al thing that ye see. …

Go forth, king, rule thee by sapience;
Bishop, be able to minister doctryne;
Lord, to trew consayl yeve audience; …

O mortal folk, you may behold and see
  How I lie here, sometime a mighty knight;
The end of joy and all prosperitee …

Forget not yet the tried intent
Of such a truth as I have meant;
My great travail so gladly spent, …

Sing lullaby, as women do,
  Wherewith they bring their babes to rest;
And lullaby can I sing too, …

Hey! now the day dawis;
The jolly cock crawis;
Now shroudis the shawis …

Passions are liken’d best to floods and streams:
  The shallow murmur, but the deep are dumb;
So, when affection yields discourse, it seems …

MOST glorious Lord of Lyfe! that, on this day,
Didst make Thy triumph over death and sin;
And, having harrowd hell, didst bring away …

Long-while I sought to what I might compare
Those powrefull eies which lighten my dark spright;
Yet find I nought on earth, to which I dare …

One day I sought with her hart-thrilling eies
To make a truce, and termes to entertaine;
All fearlesse then of so false enimies, …

Penelope, for her Ulisses sake,
Deviz’d a web her wooers to deceave;
In which the worke that she all day did make, …

Sweet is the rose, but growes upon a brere;
Sweet is the iunipeer; but sharpe his bough;
Sweet is the eglantine, but pricketh nere; …

Tell me, when shall these wearie woes have end;
Or shall their ruthlesse torment never cease,
But al my days in pining languor spend, …

Mark when she smiles with amiable cheare,
And tell me whereto can ye lyken it;
When on each eyelid sweetly doe appeare …

Shall I then silent be, or shall I speake?
And if I speake, her wrath renew I shall;
And if I silent be, my hart will breake, …

Trust not the treason of those smyling lookes,
Untill ye have their guylefull traynes well tryde;
For they are lyke but unto golden hookes, …

Fayre is my Love, when her fayre golden haires
With the loose wynd ye waving chance to marke;
Fayre, when the rose in her red cheekes appeares, …

Let not one sparke of filthy lustfull fyre
Breake out, that may her sacred peace molest;
Ne one light glance of sensuall desyre …

Weep not, my wanton, smile upon my knee;
When thou art old there’s grief enough for thee.
      Mother’s wag, pretty boy, …

The loppèd tree in time may grow again,
Most naked plants renew both fruit and flower;
The sorest wight may find release of pain, …

There is a garden in her face
  Where roses and white lilies blow;
A heavenly paradise is that place, …

  Rose-cheek’d _Laura_, come;
Sing thou smoothly with thy beauty’s
Silent music, either other …

Of Neptune’s empire let us sing,
At whose command the waves obey;
To whom the rivers tribute pay, …

I ask but right, let her that caught me late,
    Either love, or cause that I may never hate;
    I crave too much--would she but let me love her; …

In summer's heat, and mid-time of the day,
    To rest my limbs upon a bed I lay;
    One window shut, the other open stood, …

All lovers war, and Cupid hath his tent;
    Attic, all lovers are to war far sent,
    What age fits Mars, with Venus doth agree; …

Leave colouring thy tresses, I did cry;
    Now hast thou left no hairs at all to dye.
    But what had been more fair had they been kept? …

I mean not to defend the scapes of any,
    Or justify my vices being many;
    For I confess, if that might merit favour, …

GrÊcinus (well I wot) thou told'st me once,
    I could not be in love with two at once;
    By thee deceived, by thee surprised am I, …

The lofty pine, from high Mount Pelion raught,
    Ill ways by rough seas wondering waves first taught;
    Which rashly 'twixt the sharp rocks in the deep, …

Fool, if to keep thy wife thou hast no need,
    Keep her from me, my more desire to breed;
    We scorn things lawful; stolen sweets we affect; …

I sit not here the noble horse to see;
    Yet whom thou favour'st, pray may conqueror be.
    To sit and talk with thee I hither came, …

If Thetis and the Morn their sons did wail,
    And envious Fates great goddesses assail;
    Sad Elegy, thy woful hairs unbind: …

Come were the times of Ceres' sacrifice;
    In empty bed alone my mistress lies.
    Golden-haired Ceres crowned with ears of corn, …

Long have I borne much, mad thy faults me make;
    Dishonest love, my wearied breast forsake!
    Now have I freed myself, and fled the chain, …

Look in thy glass and tell the face thou viewest
Now is the time that face should form another;
Whose fresh repair if now thou not renewest, …

As fast as thou shalt wane, so fast thou grow'st,
In one of thine, from that which thou departest;
And that fresh blood which youngly thou bestow'st, …

When I do count the clock that tells the time,
And see the brave day sunk in hideous night;
When I behold the violet past prime, …

Not from the stars do I my judgement pluck;
And yet methinks I have astronomy,
But not to tell of good or evil luck, …

Devouring Time, blunt thou the lion's paws,
And make the earth devour her own sweet brood;
Pluck the keen teeth from the fierce tiger's jaws, …

A woman's face with nature's own hand painted,
Hast thou, the master mistress of my passion;
A woman's gentle heart, but not acquainted …

My glass shall not persuade me I am old,
So long as youth and thou are of one date;
But when in thee time's furrows I behold, …

Mine eye hath play'd the painter and hath stell'd,
Thy beauty's form in table of my heart;
My body is the frame wherein 'tis held, …

Weary with toil, I haste me to my bed,
The dear respose for limbs with travel tir'd;
But then begins a journey in my head …

Thy bosom is endeared with all hearts,
Which I by lacking have supposed dead;
And there reigns Love, and all Love's loving parts, …

Take all my loves, my love, yea take them all;
What hast thou then more than thou hadst before?
No love, my love, that thou mayst true love call; …

That thou hast her it is not all my grief,
And yet it may be said I loved her dearly;
That she hath thee is of my wailing chief, …

When most I wink, then do mine eyes best see,
For all the day they view things unrespected;
But when I sleep, in dreams they look on thee, …

If the dull substance of my flesh were thought,
Injurious distance should not stop my way;
For then despite of space I would be brought, …

The other two, slight air, and purging fire
Are both with thee, wherever I abide;
The first my thought, the other my desire, …

Mine eye and heart are at a mortal war,
How to divide the conquest of thy sight;
Mine eye my heart thy picture's sight would bar, …

Not marble, nor the gilded monuments
Of princes, shall outlive this powerful rhyme;
But you shall shine more bright in these contents …

Like as the waves make towards the pebbled shore,
So do our minutes hasten to their end;
Each changing place with that which goes before, …

Sin of self-love possesseth all mine eye
And all my soul, and all my every part;
And for this sin there is no remedy, …

Against my love shall be as I am now,
With Time's injurious hand crush'd and o'erworn;
When hours have drain'd his blood and fill'd his brow …

When I have seen by Time's fell hand defac'd
The rich-proud cost of outworn buried age;
When sometime lofty towers I see down-raz'd, …

Those parts of thee that the world's eye doth view
Want nothing that the thought of hearts can mend;
All tongues, the voice of souls, give thee that due, …

That thou art blam'd shall not be thy defect,
For slander's mark was ever yet the fair;
The ornament of beauty is suspect, …

So are you to my thoughts as food to life,
Or as sweet-season'd showers are to the ground;
And for the peace of you I hold such strife …

Thy glass will show thee how thy beauties wear,
Thy dial how thy precious minutes waste;
These vacant leaves thy mind's imprint will bear, …

Whilst I alone did call upon thy aid,
My verse alone had all thy gentle grace;
But now my gracious numbers are decay'd, …

Or I shall live your epitaph to make,
Or you survive when I in earth am rotten;
From hence your memory death cannot take, …

I never saw that you did painting need,
And therefore to your fair no painting set;
I found, or thought I found, you did exceed …

Then hate me when thou wilt; if ever, now;
Now, while the world is bent my deeds to cross,
Join with the spite of fortune, make me bow, …

But do thy worst to steal thyself away,
For term of life thou art assured mine;
And life no longer than thy love will stay, …

Some say thy fault is youth, some wantonness;
Some say thy grace is youth and gentle sport;
Both grace and faults are lov'd of more and less: …

My love is strengthen'd, though more weak in seeming;
I love not less, though less the show appear;
That love is merchandiz'd, whose rich esteeming, …

Your love and pity doth the impression fill,
Which vulgar scandal stamp'd upon my brow;
For what care I who calls me well or ill, …

Since I left you, mine eye is in my mind;
And that which governs me to go about
Doth part his function and is partly blind, …

Like as, to make our appetite more keen,
With eager compounds we our palate urge;
As, to prevent our maladies unseen, …

'Tis better to be vile than vile esteem'd,
When not to be receives reproach of being;
And the just pleasure lost, which is so deem'd …

O thou, my lovely boy, who in thy power
Dost hold Time's fickle glass, his fickle hour;
Who hast by waning grown, and therein show'st …

In the old age black was not counted fair,
Or if it were, it bore not beauty's name;
But now is black beauty's successive heir, …

My mistress' eyes are nothing like the sun;
Coral is far more red, than her lips red:
If snow be white, why then her breasts are dun; …

Thou art as tyrannous, so as thou art,
As those whose beauties proudly make them cruel;
For well thou know'st to my dear doting heart …

Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy 'Will,'
And 'Will' to boot, and 'Will' in over-plus;
More than enough am I that vex'd thee still, …

O! call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye, but with thy tongue: …

Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain;
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express …

In faith I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise, …

My love is as a fever longing still,
For that which longer nurseth the disease;
Feeding on that which doth preserve the ill, …

O me! what eyes hath Love put in my head,
Which have no correspondence with true sight;
Or, if they have, where is my judgement fled, …

In loving thee thou know'st I am forsworn,
But thou art twice forsworn, to me love swearing;
In act thy bed-vow broke, and new faith torn, …

Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still;
My better angel is a man right fair, …

Venus, with young Adonis sitting by her
Under a myrtle shade, began to woo him;
She told the youngling how god Mars did try her, …

Crabbed age and youth cannot live together:
Youth is full of pleasance, age is full of care;
Youth like summer morn, age like winter weather; …

Beauty is but a vain and doubtful good,
A shining gloss that vadeth suddenly;
A flower that dies when first it 'gins to bud; …

Good night, good rest. Ah, neither be my share:
She bade good night that kept my rest away;
And daff'd me to a cabin hang'd with care, …

HERE lies, to each her parents' ruth,
   Mary, the daughter of their youth;
   Yet, all heaven's gifts, being heaven's due, …

FAREWELL, thou child of my right hand, and joy;
   My sin was too much hope of thee, loved boy;
   Seven years thou wert lent to me, and I thee pay, …

TO-NIGHT, grave sir, both my poor house and I
   Do equally desire your company;
   Not that we think us worthy such a guest, …

DRINK to me only with thine eyes,
      And I will pledge with mine;
   Or leave a kiss but in the cup, …

He entered well, by virtuous parts,
      Got up, and thrived with honest arts;
   He purchased friends, and fame, and honours then, …

Go now, and tell out days summed up with fears,
         And make them years;
   Produce thy mass of miseries on the stage, …

It is not growing like a tree
      In bulk, doth make men better be;
   Or standing long an oak, three hundred year, …

And shine as you exalted are;
      Two names of friendship, but one star:
   Of hearts the union, and those not by chance …

IN truth oh Loue: with what a boyish kinde
Thou doost proceede, in thy most serious waies;
That when thy heauen to thee his best displaies, …

FLy, flye my friendes, I haue my deathes wound, flye;
See there that boy, that murthering boy I say,
Who like a thiefe hid in a bush doth lye, …

DEere, why make you more of a dogge than me?
If he doe loue, alas I burne in loue;
If he waite well, I neuer thence would moue; …

SWeete kisse, thy sweetes I faine would sweetely indite,
Which euen of sweetnes, sweetell sweeter art;
Pleasing consort, where each sense holdeth part, …

YEt sighes, deare sighes, indeede true friends you are,
That do not leaue your best friend at the wurst;
But as you with my brest I oft haue nurst: …

IF Orpheus voyce had force to breathe such musicks Loue
Through pores of senseles trees, as it could make them moue;
If stones good measure daunst the Thebane walls to builde, …

ONely Ioy, now here you are,
Fit to heare and ease my care;
Let my whispering voyce obtaine …

IN a groue most rich of shade;
Where birds wanton Musicke made:
Maie then young his pide weeds shewing, …

THese sorrowing sighs, the smokes of mine annoy;
These teares, which heate of sacred fire distills:
These are the tributes that my faith doth pay, …

BEhold what hap Pigmalion had to frame,
And carue his griefe himselfe vpon a stone;
My heauie fortune is much like the same, …

THe tablet of my heauie fortunes heere
Vpon thine Altare (Paphian Power) I place;
The greeuous shipwracke of my trauels deere …

SInce the first looke that led me to this error,
To this thoughts-maze to my confusion tending;
Still haue I liude in griefe, in hope, in terror, …

MY cares drawes on my euerlasting night,
And horrors sable clowds dims my liues sunne;
That my liues sunne, and thou my worldly light, …

MY Loue bound me with a kisse
That I should no longer staie;
VVhen I felt so sweete a blisse, …

PEace is the next in Order, first in end;
As the most perfect State of Government,
Where Art and Nature each to other friend, …

NOw, if the best, and choicest Government
Of many heads, be in her nature this;
How can the Democratical content, …

NOw, though I know our books are fill'd with praise
Of good mens vertues, freedoms popular;
Yet he that will not Audit words, but ways, …

WHat make these many laws, these rains of pow'r
Where with Mankind thus fetter'd is and bound;
These divers worships, which mens souis deflow'r …

FAyre is my loue, and cruell as sh'is fayre;
Her brow shades frowns, althogh her eyes are sunny;
Her smyles are lightning, though her pride dispaire; …

TEares, vowes and prayers, winne the hardest hart;
Teares, vowes and prayers, haue I spent in vaine;
Teares cannot soften Flint, nor vowes conuart, …

THose snary locks, are those same nets (my Deere,)
Where-with my libertie thou didst surprize;
Loue was the flame that fired me so neere, …

REstore thy tresses to the golden Ore,
Yeeld Cithereas sonne those Arkes of loue;
Bequeath the heauens the starrs that I adore, …

LOoke in my griefes, and blame me not to mourne,
From care to care that leades a life so bad;
Th' Orphan of Fortune, borne to be her scorne, …

O Why doth DELIA credite so her glasse,
Gazing her beautie deign'd her by the skyes;
And doth not rather looke on him (alas) whose state best shewes ye force of murthering eyes …

THou canst not die whilst any zeale abound
In feeling harts, that can conceiue these lynes;
Though thou a Laura hast no Petrarch found, …

REade in my face a volume of dispayres,
The wailing Iliads of my tragicke woe;
Drawne with my blood, & printed with my cares, …

I Must not grieue my Loue, whose eyes would reede
Lynes of delight, whereon her youth might smyle;
Flowers haue a tyme before they come to seed, …

MY Faire, if thou wilt register my loue,
More then worlds volumes shall thereof arise;
Preserue my teares, and thou thy selfe shalt proue, …

MY thoughts bred vp with Eagle-birds of loue,
And for their vertues I desirde to know;
Vpon the neast I set them forth, to proue …

AS Loue and I, late harbourde in one Inne,
With Proueths thus each other entertaine;
In loue there is no lacke, thus I beginne: …

ALexis here she stay'd, among these Pines▪
Sweet Hermitresse she did all alone repaire;
Here did she spread the Treasure of her Haire, …

THis strange Ecclipse one saies
Strange Wonders doth foretell;
But you whose Wives excell, …

NEare to this Eglantine
Enclosed lies the milke-white Armeline;
Once Cloris only joy, …

THen Death thee hath beguil'd
Alectos first borne Child;
Then thou who thrall'd all Laws …

THis is no work of Stone,
Though it seems breathlesse, cold, and sense hath non…;
But that … God which keeps …

PAssenger vexe not thy Mind
To make me mine Eyes unfold;
For if thou shouldst them behold, …

NIsa, Palemons Wife, him weeping told
He kept not Grammar rules now being old;
For why (quoth she) position false make ye, …

WHo would Perfections faire Idea see,
On pretty Cloris let him look with me;
White is her haire, her Teeth white, white her Skin, …

LIfe to give life, deprived is of Life,
And Death display'd hath Ensigne against Death;
So violent the Rigour was of Death, …

AT length we see those Eyes,
Which cheere both Earth and Skies;
Now, ancient Caledon, …

TO faire hopes to give reines now is it time,
And soare as high as just desires may climbe;
O Halcyonian, cleare, and happy Day, …

THe Acidalian Queen amidst thy Bayes
Shall twine her Mirtles, grant thee pleasant daies;
She did make cleare thy house, and with her light …

O How the faire Queen with the golden maids,
The Sun of Night, thy happy fortunes aids;
Though turban'd Princes for a Badge her weare, …

WHat course of life should wretched Mortals take?
In Books hard Questions large contention make;
Care dwels in Houses, Labour in the Field, …

IN shells, and gold, Pearles are not kept alone,
A Margaret here lies beneath a stone;
A Margaret that did excell in worth …

THe angry Winds not aye
Do cuff the roaring Deep;
And though heavens often weep, …

LEt holy David, Solomon the Wise,
That King, whose Breast Aegeria did inflame;
Augustus, Helens Son, Great in all Eyes, …

LIke to the Gardens Eye, the Flower of Flow'rs
With purple Pompe that dazle doth the Sight;
Or as among the lesser Gems of Night, …

Oh my blacke Soule! now thou art summoned
By sicknesse, deaths herald, and champion;
Thou art like a pilgrim, which abroad hath done …

Batter my heart, three person'd God; for, you
As yet but knocke, breathe, shine, and seeke to mend;
That I may rise, and stand, o'erthrow mee, 'and bend …

Once, and but once found in thy company,
All thy suppos'd escapes are laid on mee;
And as a thiefe at barre, is question'd there …

Here take my Picture, though I bid farewell;
Thine, in my heart, where my soule dwels, shall dwell.
'Tis like me now, but I dead, 'twill be more …

Oh, let mee not serve so, as those men serve
Whom honours smoakes at once fatten and sterve;
Poorely enrich't with great mens words or lookes; …

SIr, more then kisses, letters mingle Soules;
For, thus friends absent speake. This ease controules
The tediousnesse of my life: But for these …

REason is our Soules left hand, Faith her right,
By these wee reach divinity, that's you;
Their loves, who have the blessings of your light, …

MAn is a lumpe, where all beasts kneaded bee,
Wisdome makes him an Arke where all agree;
The foole, in whom these beasts do live at jarre, …

But you are over-blest. Plenty this day
Injures; it causeth time to stay;
The tables groane, as though this feast …

LOoke to mee faith, and looke to my faith, God;
For both my centers feele this period.
Of waight one center, one of greatnesse is; …

SLeep sleep old Sun, thou canst not have repast
As yet, the wound thou took'st on friday last;
Sleepe then, and rest; The world may beare thy stay, …

Twice or thrice had I loved thee,
Before I knew thy face or name;
So in a voice, so in a shapelesse flame, …

MArke but this flea, and marke in this,
How little that which thou deny'st me is;
It suck'd me first, and now sucks thee, …

WHo ever guesses, thinks, or dreames he knowes
Who is my mistris, wither by this curse;
His only, and only his purse …

SHee'is dead; And all which die
To their first Elements resolve;
And wee were mutuall Elements to us, …

THou art not so black, as my heart,
Nor halfe so brittle, as her heart, thou art;
What would'st thou say? shall both our properties by thee bee spoke, …

TAke heed of loving mee,
At least remember, I forbade it thee;
Not that I shall repaire my'unthrifty wast …

LAnguage thou art too narrow, and too weake
To ease us now; great sorrow cannot speake;
If we could sigh out accents, and weepe words, …

YOu that are she, and you that's double shee,
In her dead face, halfe of your selfe shall see;
Shee was the other part, for so they doe …

NO Lover saith, I love, nor any other
Can judge a perfect Lover;
Hee thinkes that else none can or will agree, …

IN what torne ship soever I embarke,
That ship shall be my embleme of thy Arke;
What sea soever swallow mee, that flood …

ALl is not well when such a one as I
Dare peepe abroad, and write an Elegie;
When smaller Starres appeare, and give their light, …

THou, whose sweet youth and early hopes inhance
Thy rate and price, and mark thee for a treasure;
Hearken unto a Verser, who may chance …

OH all ye, who passe by, whose eyes and minde
To worldly things are sharp, but to me blinde;
To me, who took eyes that I might you finde: …

AS he that sees a dark and shadie grove,
Stayes not, but looks beyond it on the skie;
So when I view my sinnes, mine eyes remove …

LOrd, I confesse my sinne is great;
Great is my sinne: Oh! gently treat
With thy quick flow'r, thy moment anie bloom; …

IMmortall Love, authour of this great frame,
Sprung from that beautie which can never fade;
How hath man parcel'd out thy glorious name, …

LIsten sweet Dove unto my song,
And spread thy golden wings in me;
Hatching my tender heart so long, …

WHen my devotions could not pierce
Thy silent eares;
Then was my heart broken, as was my verse: …

I Know the wayes of learning; both the head
And pipes that feed the presse, and make it runne;
What reason hath from nature borrowed, …

I Cannot skill of these thy wayes.
Lord, thou didst make me, yet thou woundest me;
Lord, thou dost wound me, yet thou dost relieve me: …

POore silly soul, whose hope and head lies low;
Whose flat delights on earth do creep and grow;
To whom the starres shine not so fair, as eyes; …

AWake sad heart, whom sorrow ever drowns;
Take up thine eyes, which feed on earth;
Unfold thy forehead gather'd into frowns: …

AS men, for fear the starres should sleep and nod,
And trip at night, have spheres suppli'd;
As if a starre were duller then a clod, …

WHen God at first made man,
Having a glasse of blessings standing by;
Let us (said he) poure on him all we can: …

HOw fresh, O Lord, how sweet and clean
Are thy returns! ev'n as the flowers in spring;
To which, besides their own demean, …

AH my deare angrie Lord,
Since thou dost love, yet strike;
Cast down, yet help afford; …

THe harbingers are come. See, see their mark;
White is their colour, and behold my head.
But must they have my brain? must they dispark …

COme ye hither all, whose taste
Is your waste;
Save your cost, and mend your fare. …

WElcome sweet and sacred cheer,
Welcome deare;
With me, in me, live and dwell: …

KIng of glorie, King of Peace,
With the one make warre to cease;
With the other blesse thy sheep, …

IN natures peeces still I see
